<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">29 March in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">6 events that happened on 29 March, from 1461 to 1973.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1461 CE <span class="tih-ago">565 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Yorkists win the Battle of Towton</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Fought in a snowstorm on Palm Sunday in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>, Towton is often called the bloodiest battle on English soil, with traditional estimates of 28,000 dead. It secured the throne for Edward IV.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1638 CE <span class="tih-ago">388 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Swedish settlers found New Sweden on the Delaware</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Colonists from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalmar-nyckel">Kalmar Nyckel</a> and Fogel Grip landed at the site of Wilmington and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-christina">Fort Christina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1655, but its log cabins became an American tradition.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1848 CE <span class="tih-ago">178 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Ice jam stops Niagara Falls</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Ice blocking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-river">Niagara River</a> at Lake Erie reduced the falls to a trickle for over a day. Sightseers walked on the riverbed; it is the only time the falls are known to have stopped naturally.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1867 CE <span class="tih-ago">159 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Queen Victoria signs the British North America Act</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The act united the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> and New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of Canada. Confederation took effect on 1 July, now Canada Day.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soviet-union.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1951 CE <span class="tih-ago">75 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Julius and Ethel Rosenberg convicted of espionage</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> jury found the couple guilty of conspiring to pass atomic secrets to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Their execution in 1953 remained controversial, especially over Ethel's role.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1973 CE <span class="tih-ago">53 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Last US combat troops leave South Vietnam</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The final American combat units departed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-accords">Paris Peace Accords</a>, and the last prisoners of war were released. Some 58,000 Americans and millions of Vietnamese had died in the war.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li></ol>
